    "evidence_text": "Overexpression of the proto-oncogene bcl-2 promotes abnormal cell survival by inhibiting apoptosis.\n\nHuR knockdown also leads to redistribution of bcl-2 mRNA from polysomes to monosomes.\n\nSuppression of intestinal neoplasia by deletion of Dnmt3b\n\nNevertheless, we observed a significant decrease in the formation of macroscopic colonic adenomas.\n\nClinically, primary human melanoma expression microarrays revealed tight nearest neighbor linkage for MITF and BCL2.\n\nThese data suggest that malignant transformation by BCL-6 involves inhibition of differentiation and enhanced proliferation.\n\nLin28b is sufficient to drive liver cancer and necessary for its maintenance in murine models.\n\nThus, mutation of beclin 1 or other autophagy genes may contribute to the pathogenesis of human cancers.\n\nInstead, apoptosis was attenuated and distributed along the length of the crypt.\n\nTogether, these findings suggest that deregulation of BCL9 is an important contributing factor to tumor progression.\n\nAntiapoptotic BCL-2 is required for maintenance of a model leukemia.\n\nHowever, it remains uncertain whether apoptotic defects are essential for tumor maintenance.\n\nLoss of this single molecule resulted in cell death, despite or perhaps attributable to the presence of other oncogenic events.",
